# -*- mode: python-mode; -*-
#
# Build the indices
# Read the single index file and extract the different index entries
# There are several indices:
#   TERM - General index
#   CONST - MPI Constants and predefined handles
#   EXAMPLES - Examples
#   NEWEXAMPLES - Examples with a special format; see below
#   TYPEDEF - MPI C typedefs
#   <null> - MPI Functions
# Each of these has a list:
#   listTERM
#   listCONST
#   listEXAMPLES
#   listTYPEDEF
#   listFUNCS
# In addition, we keep track of info and string constants
#   listINFOCONST_KEY
#   listINFOCONST_VAL
#   listSTRINGCONST
# We do this by permitting an optional sublist specifier for the index type
# using the format <NAME>(=TYPE), for example INFOCONST=KEY and INFOCONST=VAL
#
# Idea here is to capture constants in categories that change as the standard
# evolves - things like status fields aren't included because they rarely change
#
# todo - listERRCONST       - MPI_ERR and MPI_T_ERR
#            Update \error, \errormain to use \IIconstidx[ERRCONST]{...}
#            (Could also, or instead, check for MPI_ERR_... and MPI_T_ERR_...)
#      - listSTRINGMAXSIZE  - MPI_MAX_
#            Update this script to detect these as CONST (MPI_MAX_....)
#      - listDATATYPE       - MPI datatypes (fairly stable now, and in
#        		      many subcategories)
#            Update \mpidtype etc to use \IIconstidx[DTYPECONST]{...}
#      - listSPLITTYPE      - Few types, but being added to
#      - listMODES          - MPI_MODE
#            Update this script to detect these as CONST (MPI_MODE_...)
#      - listPVARCLASS      - MPI_T_PVAR_CLASS (fairly stable, may not need to
#                             managed in this way)
#            If done, update this script to detect these as CONST
#            (MPI_T_PVAR_CLASS_...)

